<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#cyzicene">Glossary</a> · Term · currency</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cyzicene</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Cyzicene stater</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cyzicenes were electrum coins struck by the Greek city of Cyzicus on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-of-marmara">Sea of Marmara</a> from the 6th to the 4th century BCE. Usually staters of about 16 grams, marked with the city's tuna-fish emblem beneath a changing main design, they were among the most widely used coins in the trade of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cyzicene.jpg" alt="Cyzicene" width="320" height="243" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: G.dallorto · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Istanbul_-_Museo_archeol._-_Auriga_greco_-_Arcaico,_sec._VI_aC,_da_Cizico_-_Foto_G._Dall%27Orto_28-5-2006.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
